						"boxes" : [ 							{
								"box" : 								{
									"id" : "obj-71",
									"linecount" : 30,
									"maxclass" : "comment",
									"numinlets" : 1,
									"numoutlets" : 0,
									"patching_rect" : [ 1012.5, 429.0, 791.5, 420.0 ],
									"text" : "To run this patch, simply turn on the volume- nothing outside the gen~ was edited at all.\n\nFor this assignment, I decided to create a robot band with multiple synthesized and differently in and out of sync instruments.  I based my sound off of 3 patches from the go book- string_pitched (using the string gen~), euclidian_rhythms (speicfically the drum gen~), and shiftreg-gated-comparator (specifically the synths gen~).  The string works as a robot guitarist, the drum works as a robot drummer, and the synths work as a robot bassist.\n\nAs for how I used the different params, how I changed each patch to fit these params (as they work in tandem), and why, I used the bar ramp to control the change of the note the bass plays, so it picks a new note every bar, and I used the beat ramp to control when the bass plays a note, playing a note every beat.  This creates a very smooth and rhythmic bass line, that working with the other params (which I will explain in order), is different every point in time.\n\nThe seconds param is used to make a BPM for the guitar, creating an effect where the BPM ramps up throughout the minute, making the rhythm sound idiosyncratic and upbeat.  The minute param creates a MIDI note which is then is added to a randomized MIDI note using scaled noise to create the note the guitar plays, which changes every second, using that same seconds param.  The minute param also controls the volume of the drum, making it sound like it's being hit harder or quieter.  The hour param is also used in the guitar, essentially controlling the reverberation of the string, so that it sounds like either a quick pluck of a tight string, or a stretched pull of a loose string.  The hour param also controls the MIDI note of the bass, which is also combined with a scaled noise random MIDI note.\n\nThe day param, like the seconds param, creates a BPM ramp, and this BPM is used to control the BPM of the drum and how it is hit.  The month param controls some additional params within the synth, allowing for it to change the density and create an overall different sounds.\n\nSome additional notes on how I edited the patches I added to my patch include how the synth patch didn't take ramps as input, so I had to add the delta abs > 0.5 feature in order for it to intake a specific '1' message, which I also had to do for the synth's zerox object/patcher.  The frequency control was also different inside the original patch, so I had to revamp it in order to be linked to my param controlled randomized midi note.  For many of these patches, the main way I changed them was to look at some of the default values which did not use params, and tie params to certain ranges based off of those values, such as the volume of the drum.  Some patches, like the string, used operations outside of their own gen, so I had to do some thinking in terms of how I would create those features within a gen, using different versions of objects like adding the sound and delay together.  Some of the patches' volume were not equal to each other, so I multiplied the quiter ones' outputs.  I also had to get rid of and add latches in certain places where that would effect how the patches would intake certain params and inputs."
								}

							}
